","In probability theory, a probability law $\mathcal{P}$ is a class of functions that maps a subset of a subset of a subset-dimensional subset of a set to a real number, which represents the probability of the subset. It is a fundamental concept that ensures the correctness and completeness of probability theory. 

Mathematically, the probability law is defined as a subset of the set of all functions $f$: \(\mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}\) that satisfies the following properties: 

1. **Real-Defined:** The probability level of a subset is a real number, i.e., \(\mathcal{P}(x) \equiv \mathbb{P}(x) \equiv f(x) \mod \mathbb{R}\). 
2. **Real-Defined Extension**: The probability law is a real-defined extension of the set of all functions \(f(x)\). 

This definition is mathematically rigorous but not practical for practical use. In practice, the probability law is often defined as a **real-defined function** that maps a subset of a subset-dimensional subset of a set to a real number, which represents the probability of the subset. 

