\]

The Fourier transform evaluates to:

\[
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} 3\sin(2\pi t) \, dt + \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} 2\cos(4\pi t) \,dt
\]

The integral of \(\sin(2\pi t)\) over \(-\infty\) to \(+\infty\) is zero, and the integral of \(\cos(4\pi t)\) over \(-\infty\) to \(+\infty\) is also zero. Therefore, the exact value of the integral is zero, but this is not the standard way to derive the Fourier transform representation. The Fourier transform is defined as:

\[
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(t) \, d\theta = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(t) \, d\theta
\]

The Fourier transform is given by:

\[
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(t) \, d\tau = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \left( 3\sin(2\pi t) + 2\cos(4\pi t) \right) e^{-2\pi t \xi} \, d\tau
\]

