What is the decay length of the evanescent wave in a GaAs core and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As cladding, where \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), for a wavelength of 1 μm?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The decay length of an evanescent wave is given by:
\[
\Delta x = \frac{2\pi}{k_F} \left( n_{\text{film}} - n_{\text{substrate}} \right)
\]
where:
- \(k_F\) is the wave number in free medium (e.g., air or air)
- \(n_{\text{film}}\) is the refractive index of the film (GaAs)
- \(n_{\text{substrate}}\) is the refractive index of the substrate (Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{.8}\)As)

Given:
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\). Here, \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective index of the waveguide, and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the film (GaAs).
- \(\lambda = 1 \, \mu m = 10^9 \, \text{m}\).

First, we need to find the refractive indices \(n_{\text{film}}\) and \(n_{\text{substrate}}\). For GaAs and Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As, typical refractive indices at 1 μm are approximately:
- \(n_{\text{film}} = n_{\text{GaAs}} \approx 1.45\)
- \(n_{\text{substrate}} = n_{\text{Ga}_{0.2}\text{Al}_{0.8}\text{As}} \approx 1.10\)

Using the given condition \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \approx 0.3\), we can find \(n_{\text{eff}}\):
\[
n_{\text{eff}} = n_f + 0.3 = 1.45 + 0.3 = 1.75
\]

Now, we can find \(k_F\):
\[
k_F = \frac{2\pi}{k} = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda} = \frac{2\pi}{10^9 \, \text{m}}
\]

Substituting the values into the decay length formula:
\[
\Delta x = \frac{2\pi}{k_F} \left( n_{\text{film}} - n_{\text{substrate}} \right) = \frac{2\pi}{\frac{2\pi}{10^9 \, \text{m}}} \left( 1.45 - 1.10 \ \text{cm} \right)
\]
\[
\Delta x = 10^9 \, \text{m} \left( 0.35 \right) = 350 \, \text{mm} = 350 \, \text{mm}
\]

Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{350 \, \text{mm}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
