1. **Thermal de Broglie wavelength \(\lambda\)**:
The thermal de Broglie wavelength is given by:
\[
\lambda = \frac{h}{m_0 \sqrt{2kT}}
\]
where \(h\) is Planck's constant (\(6.626 \times 10^{-34}\) J·s), \(m_0\) is the mass of one molecule, and \(T\) is the temperature.

The mass of one \(^4\text{He}\) molecule is:
\[
m_0 = \frac{6.022 \times 10^{-26} \text{ kg/mol}}{6.022 \times 10^{23} \text{ molecules/mol}} = 1.007 \times 10^{-26} \text{ kg}
\]
Substituting the values:
\[
\lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J·s}}{(1.007 \times 10^{-26} \text{ kg} \times \sqrt{2 \times 1.380 \times 10^{-23} \text{ J/K} \times 290 \text{ K}} = 1.007 \times 10^{-26} \times 1..6 \times 10^{-19})}
\]
\[
\lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{1.007 \times 10^{-26} \times 1..1 \times 10^{-19}} \approx 5.9 \times 10^{-10} \text{ m}
\]

2. **Free Helmholtz factor (\(\exp(\mu/kT)\))**:
For an ideal gas, the molar volume \(V\) is:
\[
V = nRT/p = \frac{1 \times 8.314 \times 290}{1} = 2400 \text{ cm}^3 = 2.4 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}^3
\]
